WT-06: response time of the extra pull after a sudden baryon change
  criteria f11ca13c3; kappa = 1/2 FITTED; footings never pooled; Z = 5.7888; not theory closed; MUTATE=False; SMOKE=False
  footings: canonical a0 = 9.3603e-11, alt a0 = 1.1312e-10 m/s^2

(a) DERIVATION (sympy)
  S0 D_full = u + R(u), R = u^2/(a0-u)                         : True
  S1 chi' = G M_c(<r)/r^2 solves D^2 chi = K rho_c; no M_b in it : True / True  (Phi: True)
  S2 parent force on a medium marker = R(u) - g_N              : True
  S3 omega^2 = 2 u R'(u)/r + dg_N/dr; R' = u(2a0-u)/(a0-u)^2     : True / True
  S3 deep point mass: omega^2 = 2 g_N/r, (omega t_dyn)^2 = 2 g_N/g: True / True
  S4 drift rate Gamma = alpha tau 4 pi G rho_ph                 : True
  S5 parent + drift: s^2 + Gamma s + omega^2 = 0                 : True
  S7 gradual loss over T: peak residual x (1 - e^-GT)/(G T)      : True
  Reading: the extra pull on baryons and light is u = chi' = G M_c(<r)/r^2, the medium's own Newtonian pull. chi has no
  baryon source, so at t = 0+ the extra pull is EXACTLY unchanged; only the medium's motion can change it. The field part
  (Phi, chi) is a constraint: Phi re-forms at c (time r/c), chi instantly in the medium frame. The mass part moves under
  R(u) - g_N: self-gravity cancelled, restoring force from the baryons only.

